{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/326766-looking-for-advice-on-properly-mapping-out-philadelpha-for-a-hunter-campaign","id":326766,"title":"Looking for advice on properly mapping out Philadelpha for a Hunter campaign","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Hunter: The Vigil"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-12-01T02:39:27-08:00","id":326766,"title":"Looking for advice on properly mapping out Philadelpha for a Hunter campaign","content":"I've been running a fairly decent (at least in my opinion) Hunter: The Vigil game for quite a while now, and I'm looking to expand on it significantly. Make the city feel more real, mysterious, and a bit more &quot;alive&quot; (or dead/undead as the case may be).<br /> <br /> While the player character's cell has traveled to various locations across the globe at this point, the main setting is in the city of Philadelphia, and they always eventually head back to there. I've been trying to flesh Philly out using Damnation City, along with Block by Bloody Block, and the section about Philly in the back of the main Hunter: The Vigil rulebook, but it's been, quite frankly, a little overwhelming. I feel like I'm maybe missing something obvious, or going about things the wrong way.<br /> <br /> I was considering getting an actual map of Philadelphia, then sectioning it out into factional areas/territories, and mapping it according to what is currently written in the published nWoD sources, as applicable, then filling in the blanks with my own creations, then creating a separate map for the players to basically &quot;uncover/chart/keep track of territory and haunts&quot; on their own. On a side note I was also considering doing a short Vampire: The Requiem game as a miniseries that would exist in the same place at the same time, and slightly influence the events of the Hunter arc in certain ways (just to shake things up), but that may be too ambitious at the moment.<br /> <br /> However, I am not all that familiar with Philly in real life (never been there), and I've been having issues finding a decent city map, or at least one that would work with the given material. I might just be looking in the wrong place.<br /> <br /> It's been hard to get a good mental overview of the mortal and supernatural factions that exist in Philly, where they live, and what they control, and why. Well, at least for me.<br /> <br /> If anyone has any advice, guidance, resources, information, or ideas, I'd much appreciate it. Thank you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/819-vaneheart","name":"Vaneheart","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar819_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-01T03:43:18-08:00","id":326816,"title":"","content":"I just have two general pieces of advice for you:<br /> <br /> Do not worry about sticking as close as possible to &quot;canon&quot; Philly. Do with the city what works best for your Chronicle and players. Use existing resources where you find it appropriate, but do not feel constrained by it.<br /> <br /> In the WoD, the narrative is more important than the factual reality. Feel free to change or adapt &quot;your&quot; Philly to the needs of the Chronicle. Subtle and not so subtle changes can compared to real life Philadelphia can actually enrich the story.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Politeness is the lubricant of social intercourse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","name":"saibot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar272_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1063"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-04T11:21:04-08:00","id":330356,"title":"","content":"Keep it simple. Forget about all the neighbourhoods. Just divide it in districts. Make sure each district brings something new to the table. West is university, Center is central business district, North is the slums, South is working class industrial, North West is upper class and North East is suburbs. Here is a good <a href=\"http://wikitravel.org/en/Philadelphia\" target=\"_blank\">link</a> with a good map to wikitravel. They split up center in three parts here. Center West (CBC), Center East (City Hall) and Oldtown (historical). The most important stuff is usually central. <br /> <br /> After this you are good to fill it up with buildings.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1873-jan-paparazzi","name":"Jan Paparazzi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1873_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"257"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}